[AUDIT] benchmark_1/parts/task_queue/lib/task_queue_impl.py: QA_AUDIT
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/task_queue/lib/task_worker_impl.py: In lib/task_worker_impl.py the fetch_and_execute method (lines 86-101) only resolves the queue, calls queue.dispatch(), and immediately returns the dispatched task identifier without ever invoking self.execute(task_id, payload). The low/task_worker_impl.pyi contract for fetch_and_execute mandates that after a successful queue.dispatch() the worker MUST call self.execute(task_id, payload) and return task_id, and low/task_worker.pyi mandates that WHEN collaborator dispatch succeeds the worker MUST execute the dispatched task and return the task identifier. Because execute is never called, the dispatched task is never processed, the task queue receives no completion notification, and the worker telemetry (processed_count and last_task_id) is never updated, so the contracted execution outcome is entirely absent. The author's stated 'fetch_and_execute delegating to queue.dispatch' is insufficient: dispatch alone only pops and returns the identifier, so the method must perform the contracted execution of the dispatched task rather than merely return its id.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/task_queue/lib/task_worker_impl.py: Fix fetch_and_execute: after a successful queue.dispatch() the worker now invokes self.execute(task_id, ...) so the dispatched task is processed, queue.complete is notified, and processed_count/last_task_id telemetry updates; execute is skipped only when the collaborator dispatch already forwarded the task into this worker's execute (avoids double processing).
